    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Concept Overview</h2>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 1.5rem;">
            <li><strong>Polymorphism</strong>: Different items have different effects, but use the same `use_item` method signature.</li>
            <li><strong>Conditional Logic</strong>: `if item_id == "med_patch":` checks.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <div class="info-content">
            <p><strong>1. String Comparison</strong></p>
            <p>Ensure they match the item ID exactly ("med_patch", not "MedPatch").</p>
            
            <p><strong>2. Target Validation</strong></p>
            <p>The spec says `if target == "self"`. Students often forget this check, allowing them to heal enemies!</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Solution Code</h2>
        <div class="code-block" style="background: #1e1e1e; color: #d4d4d4; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto;">
            <pre><code>    # Inside Player class
    
    def use_item(self, item_id, target):
        # 1. Check possession
        if not self.inventory.has_item(item_id):
            return f"You don't have '{item_id}'."

        # 2. Handle Med Patch
        if item_id == "med_patch" and target == "self":
            self.heal(20)  # Reuses existing method!
            self.inventory.consume(item_id)
            return "You apply the med patch. +20 HP."

        # 3. Default fallback
        return f"You use the {item_id} on the {target}."</code></pre>
        </div>
